  <h1>Comparison: Apna HTML Component vs. Alternatives</h1>\
\
  <p>Here is how this implementation compares to both the raw functional/HTML-only approach and a traditional JavaScript framework (like React or Vue), along with why it strikes a practical balance:</p>\
\
  <h2>Compared to a Plain HTML Page</h2>\
  <ul>\
    <li><strong>Interactivity:</strong> A static HTML page requires full page reloads or clunky vanilla DOM manipulation to handle multi-step form validation, state toggling, and dynamic step progression. This implementation uses a self-contained reactive component structure that updates instantly in place.</li>\
    <li><strong>Maintainability:</strong> State logic (like active steps, form inputs, and day toggles) is bundled cleanly alongside the markup rather than scattered across separate event listener scripts.</li>\
  </ul>\
\
  <h2>Compared to a Heavy JS Framework (React/Vue/Angular)</h2>\
  <ul>\
    <li><strong>Simplicity & Portability:</strong> Traditional frameworks require a build step (Webpack/Vite), node modules, and a complex deployment pipeline. This approach runs directly inside a single HTML file template[cite: 1] while retaining modern reactive data-binding and clean conditional rendering.</li>\
    <li><strong>Performance & Footprint:</strong> There's no heavy virtual DOM reconciliation or massive client-side bundle to download, making it lightweight and fast to load.</li>\
  </ul>\
\
